@article{kaplan2020seeing,
  title = {{Seeing through health information technology: the need for transparency in software, algorithms, data privacy, and regulation*}},
  author = {Kaplan, Bonnie},
  journal = {Journal of Law and the Biosciences},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {7},
  number = {1},
  pages = {lsaa062--lsaa062},
  doi = {10.1093/jlb/lsaa062},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1093/jlb/lsaa062},
  abstract = {Big data and artificial intelligence (AI) algorithmic decision-making are becoming more common in healthcare as they are in other sectors. Improvements in both data protection and access, as well as in algorithms, for purposes as different as predictive policing, assessing government performance, democratic deliberation, scientific research, news reporting, and healthcare provision are hoped for through increased data collection and access. At the same time, concerns abound about how data are generated, collected, processed, and used; and how algorithms are trained, how and if they work, and whether they actually improve care. Calls for transparency in algorithms, data, and data use have been increasing, in part, in the hopes of increasing effectiveness, accountability, and privacy. Opaqueness can thwart the promise technologies can help achieve. Rather than considering transparency an unmitigated good, or opaqueness as necessarily bad, circumstances surrounding the particulars should be taken into account so that wise decisions may be made regarding how much transparency is advisable for what purpose, and how best to achieve it. This paper sketches transparency issues in the context of health information technologies such as AI, robotics, telemedicine (including telehealth, and smart phone apps), electronic health records (EHRs), and health-related data. Concerns that surround healthcare, health-related commercial information technologies, and social media that collect user-generated data can illuminate more general information technology transparency considerations. I start with software, where general calls for algorithmic transparency are common. These healthcare information technologies are based on complex software systems comprised of numerous algorithms that are not well understood. I echo those calls for transparency, as well as indicate additional software-related transparency issues, such as those stemming from intellectual property (IP) and liability. Concerns surrounding algorithmic transparency in general, or rather its lack, apply, then, to healthcare software. Then I move to privacy and health data, emphasizing areas where privacy is compromised through lack of transparency. Throughout, I allude to contractual and regulatory issues that contribute to the lack of transparency and point toward the need for change. This survey is unusual in bringing together different aspects of the need for transparency from multiple disciplines, and for using health information technologies as the lens to do so. It draws on literature in law, medical informatics, ethics, and other domains. It suggests more generally the need for transparency in how information technologies and data are used and some ways toward achieving that. It takes an interdisciplinary approach that can lead to multifaceted recommendations for addressing these issues more effectively. Much software is protected as IP. Trade secrets or vague patents make it hard to know what exactly it does, how it does it, what data were used to create and test it, and the quality of that data as well as of software validation procedures and of output results. Even if system architecture, software, procedures, and data were disclosed, an algorithm in the software could be so complex that it is difficult even for experienced software gurus to comprehend, and nearly impossible to be assured it functions correctly. In addition to software directly coded by people, algorithms that are themselves created by software, developed through machine learning or genetic programming or other AI approaches result in software impossible to predict, and even if revealed, nearly impossible to understand. If an algorithm is ‘trained’ by humans, the nature and quality of that training, and trainers, also may be unknown. Further, it also can be very difficult to assess the underlying data.1 There are many ways, then, that it is difficult to know how and why an algorithm gives the results it does, resulting in a black box that leads to informational asymmetries so that how it was developed, trained, or works, or gives the results it does is not known to those using or affected by it. That makes assessing the algorithm and associated ethical considerations, risks, and responsibilities challenging.2 Transparency, explainability, interpretability, testability, understandability, identifiability of inherent biases or outright errors, correctability, accountability all are compromised, yet all are considered fundamental to accepting algorithmic recommendations and to human rights (e.g., the EU’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).3). Lack of transparency affects not only AI algorithms, but also complex systems, such as EHR systems for ordering, communicating, storing, and retrieving clinical information. For example, clinical record data entry and record keeping includes ordering a patient’s medications. This can involve automated dosage calculations based, in part, on the patient’s weight. The calculation may seem straightforward, a simple algorithm to compute a formula, though there can be errors due to incorrect input, malfunctioning software or hardware, erroneous formulas, inconsistencies in how to record either the weight or the medication, difficulty of reading (or even finding) the result amid other information displays, and the like.4 It can be difficult to know whether the resultant calculation is correct, especially if it is not wrong enough to be an obvious error, yet off enough to seriously harm a patient. (Consider how important even a very small error might be to a very small patient, such as a premature infant.) How well tested and vetted is the result that might be produced? How transparent is the calculation? How possible is it to examine the software, which is likely protected as IP? Clinicians are presumed to be responsible for detecting if there is an error, whether in such a formula or what information it displays. The system vendor contract may include clauses that hold the vendor harmless regardless of cause of error and instead place sole responsibility on the clinician, who is considered a ‘learned intermediary’. EHR software is not regulated by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Consequently, this software is not tested for safety and efficacy. Hold harmless clauses make clinicians liable for any errors while they are kept ignorant of information that might help them know there may be errors.5 Further, as these systems become the standard of practice, clinicians also can be liable for ignoring system warnings and alerts as well as for using them.6 To protect IP, other contract clauses may prevent a clinician who suspects or detects an error from showing the screen to others to alert them to the possible software error.7 The American Medical Informatics Association considers such practices unethical.8 Nevertheless, EHR vendor contracts are reported to contain such clauses, but the contracts, too, are considered IP, so cannot be examined to verify these claims. Even when they are available, finding these clauses can be exceedingly difficult. The vendor contract at Presbyterian Hospital of Dallas, the public hospital where the contract was obtained through a freedom of information request after the first US Ebola case was diagnosed there in 2014, was over 3000 pages. Further, because of the ‘joint and several clause’, the non-disclosure and hold harmless clauses may be buried in one of many contract footnotes.9 Contract provisions that broadly shield both the contract and the software to protect IP, in the words of the Westat report for the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONCIT or ONC), impede the ability ‘to compare different EHR technology developer systems, provide access to researchers, or even address possible patient safety concerns’.10 Transparency, and therefore, patient safety, are compromised. Now expand these concerns about such relatively simple parts as dosage calculations to the entire system. Consider AI algorithms involved in patient monitors and what alerts should be produced about, for example, a patient’s heartbeat. Consider predictive algorithms, ranging in kind from the long-used Apgar scores to assess newborns’ health and potential for infant mortality, first developed in 1952, or computer-based scoring systems, available since the 1980s, that rate severity of disease and prognosis for patients in intensive care, including the most recent ones based on machine learning. Consider the many clinical decision support systems that display anything from reminders that a patient is due a flu shot to differential diagnoses or medication and treatment recommendations. Care can be compromised if such algorithms are shrouded in secrecy. For example, it would not have been possible to discover that a widely used commercial algorithm resulted in Black patients not receiving needed care. Only when it was studied was it realized that the algorithm predicted healthcare costs as a proxy for illness, and so falsely concluded that Black patients were healthier than White ones when they were not.11 Only a few vendors dominate the market. Among the disadvantages to this monoculture is that inherent problems become widespread.12 However, among the advantages is that widespread use could allow for widespread sharing of information. That sharing, though, would have to be done informally (or by notifying the vendor) because of the contract ‘gag’ clauses mentioned above that protect IP. Moreover, whatever the faults of the ONC EHR certification system (required to meet what were, from the 2009 HI-TECH Act EHR Incentives Program, called ‘meaningful and called at more are on these systems this lead to more transparency, and (e.g., and data, software, and The nature of health data is so that health data are It is to protect privacy while data to be used for purposes such as patient care, research, and pu